# Round nodebatch (kind profile): the ws+Spark decode rate with the prompt as one batch over the node tier, so the
# residency machine runs on the node path (BatchSlots: the host serves the node's experts in the batch; residency
# `mid-p0-s1 why=place` in the smoke of 2026-11-05 06:34, prompt `kind=batch`), against ws alone at its best (plan (a),
# batch prefill, residency mid: 46.0 tok/s @ n 96 d 512 on 2026-11-05 04:05). Same binary, same lease, 4 rounds.
# [derived] plan (a) with residency: step 21.7 ms = card front ~17 ms (nsys 2026-09-24, kernels 17.36 ms of a step)
# + ~4.7 ms exposed host wait. The node arm: the smoke's host leg fell to 10-15 ms by step 40 (host_slots 101 -> 45 as
# residency admitted), under the card front, so the exposed term is the node's round trip a a layer (sparkr3-a card:
# 110..175 us) x 40 layers = 4.4..7.0 ms: step 21.4..24.0 ms -> 41.6..46.6 tok/s. The measured value is the node arm's
# tok/s; the plan (a) arm beside it is the paired reference. The residency warm-up (~40 steps of 96) is the same in
# both arms. Above the band means the host wait at plan (a) is larger than 4.7 ms or a below 110 us; below it, a above
# 175 us on this link (the pump hop, H2D, the ack).
kind: profile
question: with the prompt batched over the node tier and the residency machine on, does the ws with the Spark decode
  at, above or below ws alone at its best, and which term (the node round trip a, or the host wait) sets it?
term: tok/s @ n=96, prose P 512, depth 512, A6000 stage; the node arm --place bp with the Spark tier over tcp
  (BLOOMERY_TIER_NODES, BLOOMERY_TIER_LINK=tcp), the plain arm --place a; both under BLOOMERY_CARD_BUDGET=90G, the
  prompt as one batch (BLOOMERY_PREFILL unset), residency unset (mid on both)
unit: tok/s
predict: 41.6..46.6
condition: one binary (the nodebatch tree), every arm's load its own process; the Spark's tier_server loop up from the
  same tree, its caches dropped before the sitting, free >= 100 GB; no [cpu-busy] on a counted row; rows marked
  [cold] stand with that mark and the first clean row of each arm is read beside the mean
decide-in: parity with ws alone: the plain decode gains nothing from the node at this link; the gain path is the a
  term (the sparkr3-a profile: pump hop, H2D, ack) and the two-row pass (the draft amortizes a over k rows), and the
  prefill headline (batch pp over the node against plan (a)'s 220) is written beside it
decide-below: below 41.6: a is above 175 us; profile the link before any other lever (sparkr3-a), and read the node
  arm's host_slots against the smoke's 45..55 (a residency that did not converge reads here too)
decide-above: above 46.6: the exposed host wait at plan (a) is larger than modelled or a is under 110 us; write the
  ws+Spark headline with its interval and run the code corpus and depth 4096 next
